@* Per-column header search icon + inline input, backed by GridFilter.cs.
   One line to drop into any sortable header .cell:
   <ColumnFilterIcon Column="@key" Label="@displayText" Filter="Filter" OnFilterChanged="StateHasChanged" />
   Label is the human-readable header text (e.g. "Ville") — required, not
   optional, same EditorRequired reasoning as OnFilterChanged below: it
   backs GridFilterClearAll's chip row ("Ville: Paris ✕"), and a missed
   call site should fail the build, not silently show the raw snake_case
   Column key to a user.
   Stops its own click propagation so it can sit inside a header cell that's
   already click-to-sort (the whole .cell, or a .col-sort-label span inside
   it) without also triggering that cell's Sort.Toggle. Extracted as a
   component rather than repeating this markup in every header cell across
   every grid page (~15 pages, several columns each) — same reasoning as
   AutocompleteInput.razor being shared rather than copied per field.

   OnFilterChanged is required, not optional — confirmed live (Clients.razor,
   93,235 rows) that without it, the icon's own "filtered" state updates
   correctly (this component re-renders itself after its own event, same as
   any Blazor component) but the parent page's grid never does: Filter is a
   shared *reference*, and mutating its fields from a handler that lives in
   THIS component only schedules a re-render of THIS component, not of
   whichever page actually owns the Virtualize/@foreach reading Filter's
   state. The page looked completely unfiltered despite the icon visibly
   showing "filtered" and holding the right query. Invoking a parent-owned
   EventCallback (here, the parent's own StateHasChanged) is what actually
   asks Blazor to re-render the *right* component.

   When open, the input is an absolutely-positioned overlay with inset: 0 —
   it fills exactly the same box as its parent .cell (which has
   position: relative), nothing more. That's deliberately different from
   two earlier attempts that both leaked outside the cell's own box: a
   position: absolute popover dropped below the cell (clipped invisible by
   the cell's own overflow: hidden — needed there for header-text ellipsis
   truncation) and, after that, a position: fixed popover anchored via a JS
   getBoundingClientRect() call on open (visible, but didn't track the
   grid's own horizontal scroll, and depended on a JS round-trip completing
   before the input was usable — Badr, 2026-09-22: "the search field need
   to be in the column... sometime the search is not even working"). An
   inset: 0 overlay never exceeds its parent's box, so the cell's overflow
   never has anything to clip in the first place — no JS, no scroll
   tracking, no clipping ancestor to fight.

   Any number of columns can hold an active filter at once (GridFilter.cs,
   2026-09-28) — only the input BOX is exclusive (one open at a time, via
   Filter.IsOpen/ToggleOpen); the underlying query per column is independent
   and all currently-set columns AND together in GridFilter.Apply. *@
<span class="col-filter-icon @Filter.CssClass(Column)" @onclick="ToggleOpen" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">🔎</span>
@if (Filter.IsOpen(Column))
{
    <div class="col-filter-inline" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
        <input type="text" placeholder="Rechercher…" value="@Filter.QueryFor(Column)" autofocus
               @oninput="OnInput" @onkeydown="OnKeyDown" />
    </div>
}

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public required string Column { get; set; }
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public required string Label { get; set; }
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public required GridFilterState Filter { get; set; }
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public required EventCallback OnFilterChanged { get; set; }

    private async Task ToggleOpen()
    {
        Filter.ToggleOpen(Column);
        await OnFilterChanged.InvokeAsync();
    }

    private async Task OnInput(ChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        Filter.SetQuery(Column, e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty, Label);
        await OnFilterChanged.InvokeAsync();
    }

    // Enter closes and keeps the filter applied (same as clicking the icon
    // again); Escape clears what was typed and closes — same split as
    // Excel's own column filter box. Neither should require reaching for
    // the mouse.
    private async Task OnKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        switch (e.Key)
        {
            case "Enter":
                Filter.ToggleOpen(Column);
                await OnFilterChanged.InvokeAsync();
                break;
            case "Escape":
                Filter.Cancel(Column);
                await OnFilterChanged.InvokeAsync();
                break;
        }
    }
}
